<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!-- ============================================================================
         CRG v1 BASELINE, part 2 of 4: the tables CRG OWNS, plus the read indexes CRG
         owns on the shared response tables.

         THIS IS THE VERSION 1 BASELINE. Every DCRE database is dropped and recreated
         at the v1 cutover, so every object below is created against an empty schema
         by its single owner. The usual re-run guards are therefore absent, and the
         absence is deliberate: an onFail="MARK_RAN" tableExists guard on a table only
         CRG creates could never fire, and a guard that cannot fire is not free. It
         would silently SKIP the create on the day the assumption stopped holding,
         turning a loud startup failure into a table that is quietly missing.

         CRG NO LONGER MINTS ITS READ SOURCES. The pre-v1 changelog bootstrap-created
         nine relations it does not own, so that a clock-launched report window could
         run before the writers had ever executed: tx_header and tx_entry (CRR owns
         them, R-04 single writer), validation_log (CTV), isr_resp / sbsr_resp /
         pbsr_resp (CIX / CSX / CPX) and crw_emission_group / crw_emission /
         crw_emission_member (CRW). A reader minting the writer's schema is the exact
         shared-table coupling this cleanout exists to remove, and at v1 it is no
         longer merely untidy: the owners' v1 baselines create those tables UNGUARDED,
         so a CRG mint that won the race would make every owner crashloop on "relation
         already exists". Worse, the mint had already drifted. CRR's v1 tx_entry
         carries content_hash and idx_tx_entry_arrival_content_hash; the copy CRG was
         minting had neither, so on any ordering where CRG went first the fleet would
         have run on a silently wrong tx_entry.

         The consequence is an ORDERING CONTRACT, and it is stated here rather than
         left to be discovered: CRG's migration must run AFTER the writers'. Part 4's
         views and the ix_prg_* indexes below name relations CRG does not own, and
         both a CREATE VIEW and a CREATE INDEX resolve their target when they are
         created, not when they are used. On a fresh database, CRG migrating first now
         fails loudly and immediately, which is the correct failure: it names the
         missing table instead of inventing one.

         TABLE NAMES keep the prg_ prefix (prg_report, prg_status_class,
         prg_delivery_ledger, prg_watermark, and the ix_prg_* indexes) even though the
         service is now CRG. shared/rpt reads prg_report and prg_watermark heavily and
         is owned elsewhere, so renaming them is a separate change that needs the rpt
         owner. This is recorded rather than silently carried.

         ONE column is renamed, and this baseline is the only moment it is free.
         prg_report.report_type becomes prg_report.type: a column never repeats its
         own host table's name, and report_type inside prg_report says the noun twice.
         The payments sibling already ships the corrected spelling and cites this
         column as the violation. trigger_kind, window_key, file_name and job_name do
         not repeat the table name and are unchanged. report_id on
         prg_delivery_ledger names the TARGET aggregate rather than its host, which is
         the documented exemption, so it is unchanged too.
         ============================================================================ -->

    <!-- Single-writer delta watermark (R-04): one row per (client, e2e). The unique
         key IS the full business identity, so a restarted advance rewrites its own
         row rather than duplicating it. -->
    <changeSet id="crg-v1-watermark" author="dcre">
        <!-- A-82: this changeset gained a re-run guard AFTER it had already been applied,
             which changes the enclosing checksum without changing one byte of the DDL
             below. ANY rather than a pinned value: kind-dcre-dev stored 9:ef291d3f43174d19b183f2a4d49615a4,
             and every environment that migrated at a different time holds a different
             one, so a pin is correct on exactly one database and silently wrong on the
             rest. The preConditions block is what keeps the re-run safe; the checksum
             is not load-bearing here. Same call, same reason, as prr 002-batch-metadata. -->
        <validCheckSum>ANY</validCheckSum>
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not><tableExists tableName="prg_watermark"/></not>
        </preConditions>
        
        <createTable tableName="prg_watermark">
            <column name="id" type="UUID" defaultValueComputed="gen_random_uuid()">
                <constraints primaryKey="true" nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="client" type="VARCHAR(16)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="e2e" type="VARCHAR(35)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="last_status" type="VARCHAR(32)"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="version" type="BIGINT" defaultValueNumeric="0"><constraints nullable="false"/></column>
            <column name="created_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="now()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
            <column name="updated_at" type="TIMESTAMPTZ" defaultValueComputed="now()">
                <constraints nullable="false"/>
            </column>
        </createTable>
        <addUniqueConstraint tableName="prg_watermark" columnNames="client, e2e"
                             constraintName="uq_prg_watermark_client_e2e"/>
        <rollback>
            <dropTable tableName="prg_watermark"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!-- Report registry: one row per emitted artifact, append-only (no version /
         updated_at), so the entity rides Persistable rather than the version-based
         new-ness heuristic. file_name is unique and is the restart no-op key.
         job_name is the clock-scoped trace anchor rpt v_flow_trace joins against
         agt_ops.launch_intent.job_name; VARCHAR(63) matches that seam contract. -->
    <changeSet id="crg-v1-report" author="dcre">
        <!-- A-82: this changeset gained a re-run guard AFTER it had already been applied,
             which changes the enclosing checksum without changing one byte of the DDL
